# Scribble Assignment Constitution

## Core Principles

### I. TypeScript First
All new code — backend and frontend — must be fully typed TypeScript. `any` is
forbidden; use `unknown` for truly dynamic values. Type definitions live in
`backend/src/models/` and are mirrored in `frontend/src/services/api.ts` only
where strictly needed for the HTTP contract.

### II. In-Memory Only, No Persistence
All game state is held in the backend's `Map<string, Room>`. No database, no
file storage, no localStorage for server state. Restarting the backend resets
all rooms. The frontend may hold local session state (participant ID, current
room snapshot) in the React store only for the duration of a browser session.

### III. HTTP Polling — No WebSockets
All real-time synchronization (lobby participant list, canvas strokes, guess
history, game status transitions) is achieved exclusively through periodic HTTP
GET polling at approximately a 2-second cadence. WebSockets, Server-Sent
Events, long-polling, and any push protocol are strictly forbidden.

### IV. Validation at the Boundary
Every HTTP request body and query parameter is validated with Zod schemas
before any business logic runs. Empty or whitespace-only player names must be
rejected at the API layer with a clear 400 error. The frontend must display
these error messages; it must never crash on API failures.

### V. Incremental, Scenario-Gated Delivery
Work proceeds scenario by scenario in the order defined in the README:
1. Room Setup & Lobby  
2. Game Start & Drawer Flow  
3. Gameplay Interaction  
4. Result, Restart & Final Validation  

A later scenario is not started until the current one passes manual validation
with two browser tabs.

---

## Hard Constraints (Out of Scope — Do Not Build)

The following are forbidden regardless of perceived benefit. Including any of
these in a spec, plan, or implementation is a blocking violation:

- WebSockets, Socket.io, or any real-time push mechanism
- Databases (SQL, NoSQL, SQLite) or any form of persistent storage
- Authentication, sessions, JWT, or OAuth
- Multiple drawer rotation or multi-round timers / countdowns
- Custom or random word packs (only the 5 starter words may be used)
- Spectator mode, moderation (kick/mute), room passwords, invite links
- New state-management or routing libraries beyond the starter (`zustand`
  or any new global store is forbidden unless already present)
- Deployment, CI, Docker, or hosting work
- Rewriting the starter from scratch or unjustified top-level dependencies

---

## Engineering Standards

### Backend (`/backend`)
- Routes live in `src/api/`; business logic in `src/services/`; types in
  `src/models/`.
- Use centralized error handling via the existing `errorHandler` middleware.
- Keep the in-memory `Room` footprint minimal; remove no-longer-needed fields
  when a round ends or resets.
- Run `npm run build` in `/backend` before marking any scenario complete.

### Frontend (`/frontend`)
- Use functional components and React hooks only (`useState`, `useEffect`,
  `useInterval`-equivalent, etc.).
- Complex state (room session, participant ID) lives in
  `src/state/roomStore.ts`. Do not introduce a second store.
- Styles go in `src/styles/app.css` or CSS modules. No inline style objects
  except for trivial one-off overrides.
- Run `npm run build` in `/frontend` before marking any scenario complete.

### Determinism
Game-rule outcomes (word selection, score calculation) must be deterministic
and produce the same result given the same inputs. No `Math.random()` in game
logic. Word selection uses a fixed-index calculation:
`STARTER_WORDS[participants.length % STARTER_WORDS.length]`.

---

## AI Usage Rules

- AI-generated code must be reviewed line-by-line before committing.
- AI must not introduce out-of-scope dependencies or patterns.
- Every AI-suggested change must be traceable to a specific acceptance criterion
  in `speckit.specify`.
- If the AI proposes a deviation from this constitution, the deviation must be
  documented and explicitly approved before it is committed.
- Discovery notes (`discovery.md`) must be updated whenever new gaps or
  assumptions are uncovered during implementation.

---

## Review Discipline

- Each commit must be traceable to a specific task in `speckit.tasks`.
- No commit may bundle unrelated changes.
- The scenario is only marked done after manual verification:
  two browser tabs can complete the full flow described in the scenario.
- Deviations between the spec and the implementation must be documented in
  the relevant `speckit.specify` section before the PR is raised.

---

## Governance

This constitution supersedes all other conventions. Amendments require:
1. A documented reason for the change.
2. An update to `speckit.specify` and `speckit.plan` if the change affects
   scope or behaviour.
3. A new constitution version entry below.

All PRs must include a note confirming compliance with this constitution.

